{"id":4435,"date":"2021-12-06T15:26:08","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:26:08","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4435"},"modified":"2024-12-02T13:31:40","modified_gmt":"2024-12-02T21:31:40","slug":"1-7-for-loops-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-7-for-loops-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"1.7: For Loops"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2d9a8b\" name=\"6a2213a2d9a8b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2d9e1a\" name=\"6a2213a2d9e1a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Goals:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identify the purpose of loops<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Write and execute different types of for loops<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identify when to use a for loop in a program<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identify the components of the range command<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use the range command to count up, down, and in unique increments<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Describe other ways to count and print using a for loop<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Utilize loop knowledge to create flight patterns for different types of polygons<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Explain what is a private or local variable and how they are used in loops<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><b>Step for Success:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">What are Loops?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anatomy of a FOR Loop<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First FOR Loops<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A More Efficient Square<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Challenge: Fly a Polygon<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2d9f2d\" name=\"6a2213a2d9f2d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are Loops?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When we want our drone to repeat tasks, we use loops. There are two types of loops that we will learn in this lesson and the next:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FOR<\/strong> loops: These loops are generally used to count up or down a set number of times. They are great for doing activities where we know how many times we want the drone to do that activity.<\/li>\n<li><strong>WHILE<\/strong> loops: These loops are used to perform an activity until a condition is met. They are great for activities where we <em>don&#8217;t<\/em> know a set number of times that a loop needs to run.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For example:<\/p>\n<p>FOR loop: Think back to the box mission you flew in Unit 1 (where you had to fly in a square). This is a perfect opportunity to use a for loop because we know a square has 4 sides, so we can write a loop that executes exactly 4 times.<\/p>\n<p>WHILE loop: Imagine that we wanted our drone to fly up to a wall without hitting it. We might use a while loop and the ultrasonic sensor to fly until it got to the distance from the wall that we want. We could place the drone anywhere, and it would always stop this distance from the wall. Since we don&#8217;t know the exact distance, we use a while loop.<\/p>\n<p>Loops are what we call <strong>control structures. <\/strong>They control the order that steps are performed in a program by repeating steps multiple times. Normally, our program would just follow the steps in order without repeating.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2d9fa1\" name=\"6a2213a2d9fa1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Anatomy of a For Loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Loops have two primary parts:<\/p>\n<p><b>Loop condition: <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The rule that keeps the loop running. The loop will continue to run as long as the condition is true.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong><b>Loop body: <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Where the commands to execute are placed. These are the indented lines after the loop condition.<\/span><\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Loops also contain what is called a\u00a0<strong>private or local\u00a0<\/strong>variable. This variable is used in the loop condition to keep count or track of how many times our loop has executed. Since this variable is only used for the loop, good style is to give it a very simple name like i, j, k, x, etc\u2026<\/p>\n<p>In the video below, we see the parts of a for loop and how they are written in a Python program.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]https:\/\/youtu.be\/z0WuVv4-_c4[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2da04b\" name=\"6a2213a2da04b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>First For Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we understand the parts of a for loop, let&#8217;s practice some examples! Create a new file and watch the video. You can program this in <em>\u201cPython for Robolink\u201d<\/em> in the browser or PyCharm.<\/p>\n<p><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"2.3 For Loops Practice\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" src=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/embed\/X-HkVnS6qlQ?feature=oembed&#038;enablejsapi=1&#038;origin=https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen><\/iframe><\/p>\n<p>To recap, in the video we saw that the\u00a0<strong>range\u00a0<\/strong>command has 3 inputs:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>minimum (start)<\/strong> is the value that your variable will start at.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>maximum<\/strong> <strong>(end)<\/strong> is the value that your variable will end at.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Increment<\/strong> is how much will be added to your variable every time, and decrement is how much will be subtracted from your variable every time. A positive increment counts up, a negative counts down.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Try the practice exercises below to see if you can create some more advanced loops:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Print the values 5-20 by fives<\/li>\n<li>Print all the even numbers between 100 and 200<\/li>\n<li>Print a countdown from 20 to 10 (including 10)<\/li>\n<li>Add up all the numbers from 50 to 100 (not including 100) and print the total value<\/li>\n<li>Print CoDrone EDU letter by letter<\/li>\n<li>Count and then print the number of characters (including the space) in \u201cHello World\u201d<\/li>\n<li>Print \u201cI like to fly\u201d 5 times<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2da17c\" name=\"6a2213a2da17c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>A More Efficient Square<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your CoDrone EDU to fly in a square. Remember that computers start counting at 0 and 4 is the maximum.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nfor x in range(0, 4, 1):\r\n\r\n    drone.set_pitch(50)\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n    drone.move(2)\u00a0\r\n\r\n    drone.set_pitch(0)\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n    drone.set_yaw(-50)\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n    drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n    drone.set_yaw(0)\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, try other shapes, like triangles, pentagons, and hexagons!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2da222\" name=\"6a2213a2da222\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Fly a Polygon<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, we want your drone to fly in a polygon shape of your choosing. You can choose:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pentagon<\/li>\n<li>Hexagon<\/li>\n<li>Octagon<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>To fly these properly, you will need to calculate how much you will have to turn to create the appropriate angles for each shape. The chart below demonstrates how to calculate the angles. You will need to be able to select your polygon, then make the turns so that they turn the correct amount to make the shape (approximately). For example, a square has 360 total interior degrees.<\/p>\n<p>360\/4 = 90 degrees per corner<\/p>\n<p>This means that at each turn, the drone would need to make a 90-degree turn. You would then need to determine how much yaw for how long will equate to a 90-degree turn. For the polygon you pick, you will need to do the same.<\/p>\n<p>(<strong>Hint:<\/strong> <em>figure out 90 and then use it as a ratio for your turn, for example 60 degrees is 2\/3 of 90 degrees so take 2\/3 of the time from the Yaw movement that got you a 90-degree turn, and you will have a 60-degree turn.<\/em>)[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3703&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a2da272\" name=\"6a2213a2da272\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about the concept of loops. You learned about the different kinds of loops as well as when to use those different kinds. Loops are very useful for reducing long and repetitive code, so make sure to use them as much as you can![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-7-for-loops-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2123,2118,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-4435","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-loops","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4435","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":25,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4435\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13316,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4435\/revisions\/13316"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4734"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4435"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4435"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4435"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}